s; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "West Virginia sits among the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly 21 points toward the GOP and no competitive general-election polling in the record. The picture rests almost entirely on fundamentals, which point to a comfortable Republican win in the open race for the presidency.\n\nThe main counterweight is the national climate: surveys show the president's approval underwater in 47 states, which could shave a few points off the Republican margin. Even so, West Virginia has moved sharply toward Republicans over the past decade, and nothing in the available evidence suggests a competitive contest. The state remains safely Republican, with the open-seat nature of the race doing little to change that outlook.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0132, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0348, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0377, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.036, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0348, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0374, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0384, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0335, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0352, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0348, "margin": -21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.046, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.05, "margin": -19.0}]}